<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Slower walking, knee pain on stairs and one near-fall in an independent 89-year-old physician who still teaches, with a gait speed of 0.78 m/s, a Timed Up and Go of 13.8 seconds and low grip strength on comprehensive geriatric assessment. Please advise on the cause, frailty, his medicines, falls prevention and bone health, in a plan built on what matters most to him. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Within two weeks. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>What is causing the slower gait and the near-fall, and is there anything to treat beyond the knees?</item><item>Do you agree with stopping naproxen and diphenhydramine, and what should replace them?</item><item>Is he frail, and what exercise or rehabilitation do you advise?</item><item>What falls prevention does he need under STEADI?</item><item>How should his bone health be assessed and managed?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Thank you for asking me to see Hippocrates, an 89-year-old physician who still teaches two mornings a week, for slower walking and a near-fall. The history is as in your letter. He describes knee pain on stairs and on the hill, worse at the end of the day, with brief morning stiffness, and a sense that his thighs tire before his breath does. The near-fall was at night on the stairs, on a night he had taken diphenhydramine. He has had no dizziness on standing, blackouts, vertigo, numbness, tremor or change in memory. In the two weeks since stopping naproxen and diphenhydramine he sleeps about six hours with a sleep routine, and the knees are 4 of 10 on acetaminophen. He told me what matters most: to walk into town to teach, and to stay in his own house. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Plato names him in the Protagoras as &apos;Hippocrates of Cos, the Asclepiad&apos;, a physician who took pupils for a fee, and in the Phaedrus cites his method; Aristotle calls him &apos;the Great Hippocrates&apos;. These are the earliest witnesses to him. [D]</item><item>The three ancient lives (by Soranus, a late writer, and in the Suda and Tzetzes, all written many centuries after him) say that he was born on Cos in 460 BC, that his father Heraclides was a physician of the Asclepiad guild and taught him, and that he travelled widely in Greece; the Loeb editor finds &apos;a certain amount of fable&apos; in them but nothing improbable in their broad outline. [D]</item><item>The Hippocratic Corpus is not the work of one man. The Loeb editor describes it as the remains of a library of works &apos;of various dates and of different schools&apos;, a great part of which is &apos;neither by Hippocrates himself nor by his school&apos;; Aristotle ascribed one of its treatises to Polybus, and Hippocrates is &apos;a name without writings&apos;. Which treatises, if any, he wrote is still debated. [D]</item><item>Modern medicine regards him as its founder: reviews call him &apos;the father of modern medicine&apos; and &apos;the founding father of clinical medicine&apos;, for medicine based on observation of clinical signs rather than on religious or magical beliefs. [D]</item><item>Nothing about his own health is documented in the sources read: no illness, injury or cause of death is recorded. The roster&apos;s &apos;longevity; natural aging&apos; is a lead only, resting on the disputed traditions of his age. [R]</item></list><paragraph>2 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

