<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Primary osteoarthritis of both knees (M17.0). Knee osteoarthritis by the ACR clinical criteria; no X-ray is needed for the diagnosis. With quadriceps weakness and reduced balance it is the main cause of his slower gait. Diphenhydramine at night added to the risk on the night of the near-fall. I found no orthostatic, neurologic, thyroid, B12 or visual cause. [S]</item><item>Potentially inappropriate over-the-counter medicines (Z79.1). Naproxen (chronic NSAID use at 89, with an eGFR of 63) and diphenhydramine (strongly anticholinergic) are both on the Beers list; stopping them was right. [S]</item><item>Frailty and falls. Pre-frail (Clinical Frailty Scale 3, FRAIL 1 of 5) with low grip strength; at increased risk of falls by STEADI because of the near-fall, the slow Timed Up and Go and the short tandem stance. Both are likely to improve with exercise. [S]</item><item>Bone health. A man of 89 at risk of falls, with vitamin D insufficiency; his fracture risk should be measured. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Your comprehensive geriatric assessment: gait speed 0.78 m/s, Timed Up and Go 13.8 seconds, 7 chair stands, tandem stance 6 seconds, grip 24 and 22 kg, MoCA 26, GDS-15 2, Clinical Frailty Scale 3, FRAIL 1 of 5. Blood tests at intake (eGFR 63, calcium 9.3 mg/dL, hemoglobin 13.6 g/dL, TSH 2.1, 25-hydroxyvitamin D 24 ng/mL) and vitamin B12 this morning (418 pg/mL). The medicine list before and after deprescribing. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Treat the knees as the cause of the slower gait: exercise first, diclofenac 1% gel to each painful knee up to four times a day, and acetaminophen as needed within 3,000 mg a day; no X-ray unless the picture changes (question 1). [S]</item><item>Keep naproxen and diphenhydramine stopped; replace the sleep aid with a fixed sleep routine and no sleep medicine; check every over-the-counter purchase with the clinic or pharmacist (question 2). [S]</item><item>Twelve weeks of physical therapy with a progressive strength and balance program based on Otago, then tai chi twice a week; repeat gait speed, Timed Up and Go, chair stands, balance and grip after the program (question 3). [S]</item><item>Falls prevention under STEADI: a home safety check by the physical therapist, a second stair rail and night lights, shoes with a back indoors, and a yearly eye examination; continue vitamin D3 (question 4). [S]</item><item>DXA of the spine and hips with FRAX; treat with an osteoporosis medicine only if FRAX reaches the BHOF thresholds or there is osteoporosis or a fragility fracture; vitamin D3 1,000 units daily with a recheck after about three months, and calcium from food (question 5). [S]</item><item>Keep the plan anchored on what matters most to him (4Ms), and offer advance care planning at his wellness visit. [S]</item><item>Discussed with the patient, who agreed with the plan and chose the exercise program over a walking aid, and with Dr. Vasquez-Okafor the same day. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Geriatric medicine review in a year, or sooner after any fall or if the program does not help; primary care to repeat the mobility measures after the program. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
